# Analytics invariants

## Data scoping — critical security rule
- DOCTOR role: doctorId in all analytics queries MUST be overridden server-side to the authenticated user's own ID. Never trust a client-supplied doctorId from a doctor user.
- ADMIN role only can query across doctors or supply arbitrary doctorId.
- PATIENT role: 403 on all analytics endpoints, no exceptions.
- CSV exports follow the same scoping rules as the corresponding GET endpoints.

## Materialized views
- Dashboard data comes from materialized views ONLY — never run heavy aggregation queries on the raw appointments table per request (will timeout under load).
- Exception: `new_vs_returning` monthly breakdown may query appointments directly (6-month window only).
- Views are refreshed nightly at 02:00 IST via Spring `@Scheduled`. A manual refresh endpoint exists for admins.
- If a matview is empty (first deploy, no refresh yet), return empty arrays not errors. The frontend handles empty state gracefully.

## Patient data in exports
- CSV exports include operational fields only: counts, dates, names, categories.
- Never include: medical records, diagnoses, prescription content, contact numbers/email, device readings, or triage data in any analytics export.

## Display rules
- Doctor comparison table is ADMIN only — never render for DOCTOR role even if the API accidentally returns other doctors' data.
- Retention funnel and heatmap are all-time data — never filter by date range. Show "All-time" label so the user isn't confused when changing the date picker.
- If a metric has fewer than 10 data points, show a "Not enough data yet" empty state instead of a statistically meaningless chart.

## Timezone
- All date computations use `Asia/Kolkata` (IST). Materialized views and SQL helpers use this zone; Spring DTOs pass dates as-is without re-conversion.
